---
import EnglishBreadcrumb from '../../../../components/english/EnglishBreadcrumb.astro'
import { DataSkewLab } from '../../../../components/english/DataSkewLab'
import EnglishArticleLayout from '../../../../layouts/EnglishArticleLayout.astro'
import dataSkewStylesUrl from '../../../../styles/layouts/english-data-skew.css?url'
import { getEnglishRoute } from '../../../../utils/english-routes'

const title = 'SQL Data Skew: Why One Task Takes So Long | sql.sb'
const description =
  'Learn how a hot SQL key creates a long-tail task, compare the largest worker with the average, and evaluate salting without treating a simulation as a benchmark.'
---

<EnglishArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  active="sql"
  styleUrls={[dataSkewStylesUrl]}
>
  <EnglishBreadcrumb
    items={[
      { label: 'English home', key: 'overview' },
      { label: 'SQL', key: 'sql' },
      { label: 'SQL data skew' },
    ]}
  />

  <header class="english-article__header">
    <p class="english-article__eyebrow">SQL · execution behavior</p>
    <h1 class="english-article__title">SQL data skew: why one task takes so long</h1>
    <p class="english-article__lede">
      Data skew happens when a few key values own far more rows than the rest. After a query
      redistributes rows by that key, one task can receive much more work than its peers. The stage
      then waits for the straggler, even if most tasks have already finished.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="english-answer">
    <span class="english-answer__label">Quick answer</span>
    <p>
      Check the key distribution at the exchange or shuffle that precedes the slow stage. A hot key
      can send most matching rows to one execution partition, so the slowest task—not the average—
      controls stage completion. Filter early, pre-aggregate when the operation allows it, or salt a
      hot key and merge partial results. Each choice has correctness and resource trade-offs; verify
      it with the engine's plan and runtime profile.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="english-article__body">
    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="skew-shape">
      <h2 id="skew-shape">What data skew looks like in a SQL stage</h2>
      <p>
        Consider an aggregation by <code>open_branch_id</code>. Most branch values may have a modest
        number of transactions, while one online channel accounts for a large share. A parallel
        engine often brings equal grouping keys together so it can produce one result per key:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`SELECT open_branch_id, SUM(amount) AS gross_amount
FROM transactions
GROUP BY open_branch_id;`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        If the execution plan hashes rows by <code>open_branch_id</code>, all rows for one value
        must be combined somewhere. A hot value can therefore make one task read, sort, aggregate or
        spill far more input than the others. The stage's wall-clock time is constrained by the task
        that finishes last, not by the mean amount of work.
      </p>

      <DataSkewLab client:load />

      <p>
        The lab reuses a deterministic teaching fixture: 80, 75, 92 and 680 GB across four workers.
        The salted view divides the 680 GB hot-key input into four equal illustrative buckets and
        preserves the 927 GB total. These values describe a shape only; they are not a measured
        runtime, a benchmark or a promise about what a database will do.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="find-hot-key">
      <h2 id="find-hot-key">Find the hot key before changing the query</h2>
      <p>
        Start with the rows that reach the expensive exchange, not just the raw source table. A
        simple frequency query can reveal a dominant value:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`SELECT open_branch_id, COUNT(*) AS input_rows
FROM transactions
GROUP BY open_branch_id
ORDER BY input_rows DESC
LIMIT 10;`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>LIMIT</code> syntax varies, and an exact frequency count may itself scan a large
        input. Filters, joins and earlier aggregation can also change the distribution, so compare
        the relevant input after those operations when possible. Use table statistics as a clue,
        then inspect the engine's actual plan or query profile for per-task rows, bytes, duration
        and spill.
      </p>
      <div class="english-callout" data-tone="info">
        <strong>Compare the tail with the center</strong>
        <p>
          Record the largest task's input or duration alongside the median or average. If one task
          is several times larger, an overall average can make a severe long tail look ordinary. The
          ratio is a diagnostic signal, not a universal threshold.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="mitigations">
      <h2 id="mitigations">Mitigations change the work, not just the chart</h2>
      <div class="english-compare-grid">
        <article class="english-compare-card" data-tone="right">
          <span>Reduce input before redistribution</span>
          <h3>Filter or pre-aggregate early</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Apply selective filters before rows cross the expensive exchange.</li>
            <li>Combine rows locally when the aggregate is valid at that earlier grain.</li>
            <li>Check that the rewrite preserves null, duplicate and measure semantics.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="english-compare-card" data-tone="info">
          <span>Split one dominant key</span>
          <h3>Salt, then merge partial results</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Derive a stable salt bucket from a row-level identifier.</li>
            <li>
              Aggregate by the business key plus the salt, then aggregate by the business key again.
            </li>
            <li>Expect extra keys, a second aggregation and more state or shuffle overhead.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="english-compare-card" data-tone="info">
          <span>Use engine support carefully</span>
          <h3>Check adaptive or skew-aware execution</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Some engines can split skewed work or change a join strategy at runtime.</li>
            <li>Availability and thresholds depend on the engine, version, plan and statistics.</li>
            <li>
              Confirm the executed plan; do not assume that repartitioning alone splits one key.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
      <pre><code>{`-- Derive salt_bucket from a stable row id (engine-specific hash syntax).
-- Conceptual two-stage aggregation:
WITH partial AS (
  SELECT open_branch_id, salt_bucket, SUM(amount) AS partial_amount
  FROM transactions
  GROUP BY open_branch_id, salt_bucket
)
SELECT open_branch_id, SUM(partial_amount) AS gross_amount
FROM partial
GROUP BY open_branch_id;`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Salting is not a free rewrite. It is naturally compatible with decomposable aggregates such
        as <code>SUM</code> and <code>COUNT</code>; an <code>AVG</code> needs partial sums and
        counts before division. Distinct counts, percentiles and other non-additive operations need
        their own exact or approximate merge logic. Preserve the result contract before comparing
        resource use.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="verify-change">
      <h2 id="verify-change">Verify the slowest task and the result</h2>
      <p>
        Compare the same input and query semantics before and after a change. Useful evidence
        includes maximum and median task duration, rows and bytes per task, shuffle read and write,
        spill, total resource use and the final grouped results. A flatter input chart can still
        cost more overall if it adds another exchange or aggregation.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Check whether the hot key is present after filters and joins.</li>
        <li>Compare the largest task with the median, not only with the stage average.</li>
        <li>Confirm that partial aggregates recombine to the same output at the intended grain.</li>
        <li>Repeat with representative inputs; one small test does not establish a general win.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="english-callout" data-tone="warning">
        <strong>Data skew is not the same problem as join fan-out or storage pruning</strong>
        <p>
          A join can multiply output rows when keys repeat; that is a correctness and grain
          question, even though a popular join key can also create skew. See{' '}
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('joinDuplicateRows')}>why a SQL JOIN duplicates rows</a>. A
          storage-partition filter may avoid reading files, while a hot grouping key concerns how
          work is distributed later in execution. These problems can coexist, but their fixes are
          different.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <nav class="english-article__related" aria-label="Related English resources">
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('sql')}>More English SQL resources</a>
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('joinDuplicateRows')}>SQL JOIN duplicate rows</a>
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('grain')}>Data warehouse grain</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</EnglishArticleLayout>
